[
{ type: install
  message: <<EOM
Deliverance installs a command-line launcher and an rc.d service.

Manual run:
  service deliverance onestart
  or
  deliverance

User tools:
  deliverance-nanocode
  deliverance-antares --repo /path/to/repo --cwe CWE-78
  deliverance-dead-to-rights

Configuration:
  %%ETCDIR%%/deliverance.properties
  %%ETCDIR%%/nanocode-qwen3-0.6b-jq4.json
  %%ETCDIR%%/nanocode-antares-1b.json

Documentation:
  https://github.com/edwardcapriolo/deliverance#readme
  https://github.com/edwardcapriolo/deliverance/tree/main/deliverance-antares-cli#readme
  https://github.com/edwardcapriolo/deliverance/tree/main/nanocode-deliverance#readme

The sample configuration uses the Java/Panama tensor provider because this
package installs the Maven Central jar and does not include FreeBSD native SIMD
libraries. Models are downloaded under /var/db/deliverance/.deliverance by
default.

To enable the service at boot:
  sysrc deliverance_enable=YES
  service deliverance start

Port tests:
  make test
  make test DELIVERANCE_LONGTEST=yes

The long test loads Qwen3-0.6B-JQ4. By default it reuses the invoking user's
normal Deliverance cache, usually ~/.deliverance. To force an isolated test
cache instead, run:
  make test DELIVERANCE_LONGTEST=yes LONG_TEST_HOME_DIR=/tmp/deliverance-test-home

Future FreeBSD work that may interest contributors:
  * Port the native C SIMD build for faster CPU tensor kernels.
  * Port the WebGPU/Dawn path to enable GPU decode/output-head optimization.
  * Split native acceleration into optional packages once FreeBSD libraries are
    available and tested.
EOM
}
]
